node_modules/
# The deps-content canary. bin/deps-content.mjs discovers install hooks by reading
# <root>/node_modules/<pkg>/package.json at the lockfile's own key, so the plant has to live on
# that path or the scanner cannot see it — and the rule above would hide it from git. Re-included
# for the two canary trees only; nothing under them is code, and the walking lanes still skip
# node_modules through manifests/scan-exclude-dirs.txt.
!/fixtures/scan-canary/*/node_modules/
.DS_Store
*.log
# Golden lane fixtures record a tool's .log output byte for byte; they are tracked.
!/monitor/test/fixtures/lane-capability/**/*.log
# npx download cache for the host renovate dry-run (NPM_CONFIG_CACHE target; regenerable,
# bypasses the root-owned ~/.npm) — see monitor/renovate-dryrun.mjs
/.npm-cache/
# scan outputs (SARIF/JSON can be large; treat as artifacts, not source)
# NO TRAILING SLASH. `reports` is a SYMLINK to a git-excluded directory outside the repo
# (the 16G of sweep evidence must not live in a tracked tree). A trailing slash matches
# DIRECTORIES ONLY, and git classifies a symlink as a file — so `/reports/` silently stopped
# matching the moment this became a link, and the link became visible to `git add`. Same trap
# CLAUDE.md records for /evaluations.
/reports
# artifacts a local run may drop into this dir
/tmp/
reflection.json
sbom.json
results.sarif
osv-results.sarif
# move-aside backups (monitor's pre-program/pre-mapglue convention) — kept on disk, not tracked
*.pre-program-*
*.pre-mapglue-*
# sitemap fleet manifests are regenerable artifacts (node monitor/sitemap-data.mjs).
# The S0 demo fixture (sitemap/build-fixture.mjs) is ALSO regenerable-only now (bifocal C1): it
# harvests a real client's source tree, so a committed copy is a client's structural data (file
# paths, symbol names, internal hostnames) plus whatever secrets sit in scanned config — exactly
# what happened here (a live GlitchTip DSN from vite.config.ts, rotated + purged from tracking).
# No un-ignore: it must never be re-added to the index.
/sitemap/data/*.sitemap.json
# CRA attestation private keys — NEVER commit (public .pub is committable)
cra/.keys/*.key
# The rule above is path-pinned, but CW_ATTEST_KEYDIR (cra/lib.mjs resolveKeyDir) points the keydir
# anywhere, including inside this repo, and a key placed by hand rather than by `attest.mjs keygen`
# gets no keydir-local .gitignore either. Private key material is not source under any name here:
# zero .key/.pem files are tracked or on disk as of 2026-08-25, so this ignores nothing real. A
# future legitimate one needs `git add -f`, which is the right amount of friction.
*.key
*.pem

# Generated sitemap data — regenerated on demand, never committed (portable: relative paths).
#   slices snapshots: node bin/slices.mjs snapshot <slug> --date <d>
#   mainline harvest: node bin/mainline-data.mjs <repoDir>
# client-a.snapshots.json (front-end discovery list) points into slices/, which is gitignored below
# — a committed copy is a dangling pointer on every checkout (bifocal C1).
sitemap/data/slices/
sitemap/data/mainline.*.json
/sitemap/data/*.snapshots.json

# monitor/data network caches (bifocal C5) — regenerable: CISA KEV catalogue and FIRST.org EPSS
# scores. Never track these: (1) they churn on every scan/watch run, and (2) a git-tracked copy
# means a fresh clone's mtime is "now", which used to be misread as "freshly fetched" by code that
# judged these files' currency from file mtime instead of their own content (kev.json's
# catalogVersion/dateReleased). Freshness is judged from content now; see monitor/rollup.mjs and
# cra/watch.mjs.
/monitor/data/kev.json
/monitor/data/epss.json
/monitor/data/learning.json

# Claude Code local harness config (machine-specific; not part of the platform)
/.claude/

# Generated from monitor/failure-taxonomy.json by bin/taxonomy-db.mjs --build; never the source of truth.
monitor/commitwork.db

# ── THIS OPERATOR'S FLEET, NOT THE TOOL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What a scanner FOUND, and whose code it was pointed at, are facts about the scanned party — not
# about commitwork. A public clone must carry the instrument and none of the evidence.
#
# THESE FOUR RULES WERE INERT FROM THE DAY THEY WERE WRITTEN, AND WERE MADE EFFECTIVE 2026-08-27.
# gitignore is only consulted for UNTRACKED files, so a rule added over a path already in the index
# does nothing at all — while reading, to anyone auditing this file, exactly like protection. All
# four paths stayed tracked: 7.8 MB of issue store, 55 KB of registry, and 5.3 MB of two clients'
# scan output. The block above even SAID so about projects.json ("while the file itself stayed
# tracked") — the inertness was observed, written down as prose, and left in place.
#
# What was actually done: `git rm --cached` on all four, then the stores moved into
# commitwork-sidecar (a private git repo) and are reached through the symlinks below.
#
# THE RESIDUE, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: `git rm --cached` removes a file from the index, never
# from history. Every byte these rules were meant to keep out of the repository is still reachable
# in it, and commitwork's origin is a GitHub remote, so it is still reachable there too. Making
# these paths untracked stops the NEXT commit publishing them; it does not unpublish the last two
# hundred. Actually removing them needs a filter-repo rewrite (as was done for the Co-Authored-By
# trailers) plus a delete-and-recreate on GitHub, because a force-push removes the reference and
# not the object. That is a separate decision and has NOT been taken.
#
# No trailing slashes below: a trailing-slash pattern matches directories only, git classifies a
# symlink as a file, and every path here is now a symlink. `/map/data/client-a/` would silently stop
# matching. bin/test/inert-ignore-rules.test.mjs fails if any of these goes inert again.

# The registry — real repo names, filesystem paths and deploy scoping for this operator's fleet AND
# for client work (a client's service set). monitor/projects.example.json is the shipped template.
# CW_REGISTRY overrides the path; the default is monitor/private/projects.json.
#
# The derived issue store — 54 repos' findings, 415 rows naming client repos. Generated and
# hash-chained ("never hand-edit"), so tracking it published a client's vulnerability posture as a
# side effect of version-controlling a cache. CW_ISSUES overrides the path.
/monitor/private
# ...and the OLD path it replaced. The rule above was added when the store moved; this one was not,
# so when one commit untracked monitor/projects.json (2026-08-27) and a later one re-added it (2026-09-01)
# reading the removal as an accidental deletion, `git check-ignore` matched nothing and the restore
# was silent. The live registry is monitor/private/projects.json (35 areas, in commitwork-sidecar);
# the file at this path was a stale 30-area duplicate and is NOT a symlink to it — a file symlink
# would be renamed over by the first atomic write. No trailing slash, per the block above.
/monitor/projects.json
# Same shape, found the same hour: the issue store's rule also names only the new path, and an
# unignored monitor/issues.json was sitting at the old one — untracked, so not yet published, and
# one `git add -u` from being. The declared store is monitor/private/issues.json.
/monitor/issues.json

# Client scan output held for the map viewer: client-a's CVE history and security-findings.json
# (2.3 MB, 3,719 references to their private repo names), and internal-b-dev's equivalent. This is
# another party's security posture; it is theirs to disclose, not ours.
/map/data/client-a
/map/data/internal-b-dev
# evaluations/ — the cycle artifacts. Untracked 2026-08-26 on operator instruction, ahead of
# publication, and this REVERSES a documented rule (see CLAUDE.md's documentation tiers, updated in
# the same commit): they were tracked deliberately, because parking an audit's own evidence
# somewhere untracked is a named failure mode here. That reasoning has not become wrong — it has
# been outranked. Measured 2026-08-26: 255 tracked files, 6.2 MB, naming one client in 38, its
# service prefix in 32, a second client in 15 and other projects in 25 more. An audit document
# quotes the evidence it audits, so it discloses whatever it audited; that is the property that makes these
# useful internally and unpublishable.
#
# NOT a secrets problem, checked rather than assumed: the credential-shaped strings in here are
# truncated prose (`AKIA...` describing a regex weakness, `RENOVATE_TOKEN=ghp_…` in a usage line),
# not live material.
#
# THE COST IS REAL AND IS NOT MITIGATED BY THIS LINE: the evidence for every finding this fleet has
# published now lives only on one disk. If publication proceeds as a separate public repository
# rather than by opening this one, prefer reverting this and excluding the directory THERE — that
# keeps the audit trail in version control where the failure mode says it belongs.
#
# UPDATE 2026-08-27 — the cost above is now MITIGATED, and this line lost its trailing slash for a
# reason that is not cosmetic. `evaluations` is a SYMLINK into ~/Repositories/Portll/commitwork-
# sidecar, a private git repo that versions it. A trailing-slash pattern matches only directories,
# and git treats a symlink as a FILE — so `/evaluations/` stops matching the instant the path
# becomes a link, and 306 files of client-naming audit output reappear as untracked, one `git add
# -A` away from being committed. Do not restore the slash.
/evaluations
.wrangler/

# docsite/.versions/ — the rolling overwrite-protection snapshots lib/docsite-versions.mjs writes
# (editor saves, imports, state/reorder-triggered rebuilds, the taxonomy generators). Local safety
# net, not a durable record — same reasoning /reports/ gets above. bin/docsite-publish.mjs never
# picks it up regardless (it assembles the bundle from the manifest's declared docs, not a
# directory walk of docsite/), so this line is about keeping it out of `git status`, not the
# public site.
/docsite/.versions/

# Vendored reference corpus — operator ruling 2026-09-07. None of it travels with the public
# repository; it lives in commitwork-sidecar/reference and is regenerated there. Nothing in this
# repo reads it: the apparent consumers are a docsite page name, a comment about DROPPING vendored
# components, a synthetic test fixture path, and scan manifests passing --exclude reference.
# The working copy is removed from disk too, not merely untracked — the sidecar copy is the durable
# one, verified byte-identical before either step.
# No trailing slash: git classifies a symlink as a file, so a directory-only pattern stops
# matching the moment the path becomes a link.
/reference

# operational record: the status document is written to monitor/private/ (sidecar), never tracked
/PROJECTSTATUS.md

# Customer and fleet records, moved to the private dir 2026-09-30 (monitor/private is the sidecar
# link; monitor/store-paths.mjs resolves each, CW_* overrides). The old paths stay ignored so a stray
# copy is never re-added. The *.example.json files beside them are the public shapes.
/monitor/annotations.json
/monitor/gate-exemptions.json
/monitor/image-acceptance.json
/monitor/stub-allowlist.json
# per-area editorial sidecars for build-security-data (the shared monitor/security-annotations.json stays)
/monitor/security-annotations.*.json
/monitor/owner-map.json
/monitor/program-worklist.json
/monitor/config-correctness-ledger.json
# derived from the private worklist by worklist-reconcile.mjs; written beside it, never here
/monitor/worklist-reconciled.json
# operator BOLA manifests live in monitor/private/bola/; only the synthetic example ships here. The
# two old paths are named rather than globbed: a negation that re-includes the example matches a
# tracked file, which bin/test/inert-ignore-rules.test.mjs reports as an inert rule.
/manifests/bola/client-a.json
/manifests/bola/client-d-supabase.json
# the CRA product registry is monitor/private/cra-products.json; cra/products.example.json ships
/cra/products.json
/monitor/credential-scope.json
/monitor/data/epss-detail.json

# Hand-made backups taken before the 2026-07-19 lifecycle change, removed 2026-09-30. Each live copy
# is tracked (the .mjs) or a private record (projects.json, program-worklist.json). A backup of that
# kind is never source; nothing reads one, and the walkers in bin/comment-schema.mjs and
# admin/routes/comments.mjs already skip the name.
*.pre-lifecycle-*

